WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.650
- Well, your kids are both into music.

00:00:01.650 --> 00:00:02.483
That's amazing.

00:00:02.483 --> 00:00:03.690
I love that
- They are.

00:00:03.690 --> 00:00:04.770
- Yeah.
- Though my daughter

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:08.160
just quit piano lessons.
- Oh, she did? You know what?

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:09.000
Hey, I quit piano.
- Did you?

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:10.213
- Look at me now.
- Oh, look at you.

00:00:10.213 --> 00:00:11.715
(Angela claps)
Okay. Okay.

00:00:11.715 --> 00:00:12.998
- Look at me now.
- Thank you for that.

00:00:12.998 --> 00:00:14.613
- I mean,

00:00:14.613 --> 00:00:16.290
quit and couldn't afford

00:00:16.290 --> 00:00:17.688
are two different things,

00:00:17.688 --> 00:00:18.808
I realize.
- Oh, I know that one.

00:00:18.808 --> 00:00:19.641
- Yeah, I couldn't afford.
- That's why

00:00:19.641 --> 00:00:20.474
I don't play guitar,

00:00:20.474 --> 00:00:21.657
'cause I couldn't afford.
- Yeah, I couldn't afford,

00:00:21.657 --> 00:00:23.310
that's why I quit.
- Yeah.

00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:25.290
- Like also with violin.

00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:27.030
But I quit it and look at me now.

00:00:27.030 --> 00:00:28.332
That's why.
- Okay. Okay.

00:00:28.332 --> 00:00:29.538
(audience applauds)

00:00:29.538 --> 00:00:31.043
You know why?
- 'Cause, really, I was like,

00:00:31.043 --> 00:00:33.870
"No, not everyone can play piano.

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:35.600
Oh, just," and then I flipped it.

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:37.140
It was like, "Yeah, come on down here

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:38.610
and be regular like us.

00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:40.050
Come on down here with the regular people

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:41.070
who don't play piano."

00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:42.270
- You should tell her this though,

00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:43.920
'cause I am really bummed though,

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:44.760
'cause I could play,

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:46.500
and now I try and get back at it,

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:47.333
and if you don't.

00:00:47.333 --> 00:00:48.907
Even if she's not taking lessons.

00:00:48.907 --> 00:00:50.640
- Too busy.
- Just to do it for fun,

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:52.077
because it's a beautiful thing to have

00:00:52.077 --> 00:00:52.910
when you're older.
- We want her to get back

00:00:52.910 --> 00:00:54.480
to the love and the joy of it.
- Yeah.

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:55.590
- I was like, "Hey, all right,

00:00:55.590 --> 00:00:57.390
you gotta have a passion

00:00:57.390 --> 00:00:58.320
for whatever it is."

00:00:58.320 --> 00:00:59.153
So.
- I agree.

00:00:59.153 --> 00:01:00.390
- If it's sort of waning,

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:01.223
okay.
- I love it

00:01:01.223 --> 00:01:03.000
they're into music though, that's cool.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:04.410
Yeah. It's such a fun outlet.

00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:05.850
Even if they don't do it for their career,

00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:07.470
if they wanted it for their career,

00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:08.580
I think it's a healthy outlet

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:10.200
to have something like that emotionally.

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:11.946
Or maybe it's just me
and my therapist said so.

00:01:11.946 --> 00:01:13.260
(Angela laughs)

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:14.280
- [Kelly] Whatever.

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:15.690
Do you let your kids control,

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:18.679
like whenever you get in the
car? My kids are six and eight.

00:01:18.679 --> 00:01:19.800
- I feel like I'm being ganged up on.

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:21.030
- Yeah, my kids are six and eight

00:01:21.030 --> 00:01:22.140
and they control it.
- 16 and 16,

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:25.530
so I let them, you know,
on the way to school.

00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:26.910
Yeah. Can I put on this music?

00:01:26.910 --> 00:01:28.590
My mom wants you to hear something,

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:30.210
and it's like, "Ah," the assault.

00:01:30.210 --> 00:01:31.500
Oh my gosh.

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:33.390
The words, it's too early for all that.

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:34.770
- Yeah.

00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:35.682
I know.

00:01:35.682 --> 00:01:38.370
- But I was surprised,

00:01:38.370 --> 00:01:40.230
really surprised the other day,

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:42.360
he and I, was teaching him how to drive,

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:44.700
and we were talking about relationships

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:46.980
and you know, when you
come to the end of them

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:48.660
and how to handle it, or.
- Yeah.

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:50.340
- You know, how to be on top of it,

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:52.410
like if it's coming to the end, you know.

00:01:52.410 --> 00:01:54.240
I don't wanna hang around
that sort of thing.

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:56.190
Just let me know if you're not interested.

00:01:56.190 --> 00:01:58.080
Don't keep, you know, back and forth.

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:00.120
And so he said, "Oh, ma,
I got a song for that."

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:03.420
And he presses the song and out comes,

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:04.807
Engelbert Humperdinck's,

00:02:04.807 --> 00:02:07.380
"Please release me, let me go."

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:09.330
- Oh my gosh.

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:11.670
- [Angela] Oh, and of course
I know the words to that,

00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:12.733
and he's shocked at that.

00:02:12.733 --> 00:02:14.880
I was like, yeah, "That's a oldie."

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:15.713
- [Kelly] Isn't that funny,

00:02:15.713 --> 00:02:17.160
how they think they have a new song?

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:18.187
That happens now, and I'm like,

00:02:18.187 --> 00:02:20.490
"I was like roller
skating to that, ma'am."

00:02:20.490 --> 00:02:22.110
Like, I know that song.

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:23.370
Yeah.

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:25.620
- You gotta hear all
the latest and greatest,

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:28.830
with all the words that
a mom doesn't wanna hear.

00:02:28.830 --> 00:02:30.000
Like put on Kidz Bop

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:30.990
version of that.
- Yeah. Kidz Bop.

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:32.640
Oh my God, my daughter hates it.

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:34.410
I'm always like, "You're
getting the Kidz Bop

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:35.243
version of that."
- Kidz Bop version of that,

00:02:35.243 --> 00:02:36.090
you know.
- It's so funny though,

00:02:36.090 --> 00:02:36.923
my.
- But my other play

00:02:36.923 --> 00:02:38.491
is Sinatra.
Frank Sinatra.

00:02:38.491 --> 00:02:39.840
- Oh, I love that.
- Yeah.

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:40.890
- So, I have to tell you this,

00:02:40.890 --> 00:02:42.360
but literally I'm driving my kids

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:43.193
to school every morning,

00:02:43.193 --> 00:02:44.460
and my daughter was like,

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:46.920
she likes this Dua Miley song, "Prisoner,"

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:47.753
so we play that and I'm like,

00:02:47.753 --> 00:02:49.350
"That's cool, that's
like pop, that's cool."

00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:51.187
My six year old son is like,

00:02:51.187 --> 00:02:55.080
"Can you play Ray Charles,
"Hit The Road Jack?"

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:56.670
And I was like,
(Angela laughs)

00:02:56.670 --> 00:02:58.170
I literally almost crashed.

00:02:58.170 --> 00:03:00.690
I was like, "Does he listen to me

00:03:00.690 --> 00:03:02.160
when I'm playing vinyl?"

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:04.080
'Cause I'm like, "How do
you know Ray Charles?"

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:06.030
I was like, "He's six."

00:03:06.030 --> 00:03:07.950
I was like, I'm doing
all right as a parent,

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:09.787
I feel like, when one of my kids is like,

00:03:09.787 --> 00:03:10.995
"Can you put on some Ray Charles?"

00:03:10.995 --> 00:03:12.818
(audience applauds)
It was just so funny.

00:03:12.818 --> 00:03:13.651
- [Angela] I remember

00:03:13.651 --> 00:03:15.240
when mine's were like 18 months,

00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:17.310
and I would pick my sister
up from the airport.

00:03:17.310 --> 00:03:20.160
I would play a song
because they just listen

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:20.997
and absorb everything.
- Yeah.

00:03:20.997 --> 00:03:22.470
- And so she gets in the car

00:03:22.470 --> 00:03:23.730
and I put the song on,

00:03:23.730 --> 00:03:24.800
and they start singing,

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:26.225
♪ Mr. Big Stuff ♪

00:03:26.225 --> 00:03:27.936
♪ Who do you think you are? ♪

00:03:27.936 --> 00:03:28.769
(Angela chuckles)
- Oh my God.

00:03:28.769 --> 00:03:29.970
- And she's like, "Huh?"

00:03:29.970 --> 00:03:31.200
Her mouth dropped.
- You know what though?

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:32.070
I play vinyl,

00:03:32.070 --> 00:03:33.330
I think that's where they must hear it,

00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:35.160
'cause that's where when
I play my older records.

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:37.410
So, I don't know, I'm gonna keep doing it.

00:03:37.410 --> 00:03:38.243
But I was like,

00:03:38.243 --> 00:03:39.660
that's some good music in your ear.

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:40.860
I love doing Miley too,

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:42.690
I just thought that was
more obvious for them.

00:03:42.690 --> 00:03:44.220
- Got you.
(Angela laughs)

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:45.303
- I love everyone.

